     "id": "HCA-D1",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "Is MRI with a hepatobiliary contrast agent mandatory before any adenoma is managed as an adenoma, and who may waive it?",
     "question": "Is MRI with a hepatobiliary contrast agent mandatory before any adenoma is managed as an adenoma, and who may waive it?",
     "kind": "clinical",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "1 · Confirm diagnosis and subtype",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-1"
      },
      {
       "label": "6 · Pregnancy planning and surveillance",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-6"
      }
     ],
     "positions": [
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "MRI is superior to all other modalities and can subtype up to 80% of adenomas (II-2, grade 1); contrast ultrasound separates adenoma from FNH but cannot subtype.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Standard 'Make the diagnosis on contrast-enhanced MRI…' (preferred-for and note)"
      },
      {
       "source": "AFEF 2021 (French multisociety)",
       "key": "pm34333196",
       "says": "Adds that a hepatobiliary agent has particular value for identifying focal nodular hyperplasia.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Standard 'Make the diagnosis on contrast-enhanced MRI…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "Gaspersz 2019 pregnancy cohort",
       "key": "pm31550458",
       "says": "Prospective multicentre cohort: 18 of 66 women (27%) referred with a suspected sub-5 cm adenoma had FNH on hepatobiliary-contrast MRI; the authors held that this misdiagnosis rate justifies the scan, if only to prevent unnecessary anxiety.",
       "locator": "Gate 6 · Standard 'Confirm the diagnosis on MRI with a hepatobiliary contrast agent…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "Haring 2022 resection audit",
       "key": "pm36324268",
       "says": "Of 222 Dutch resections for presumed adenoma, 43% had a changed final diagnosis; absence of hepatobiliary MRI independently predicted change (aOR 1.8), an effect the roadmap says is confounded by era and centre.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Standard 'Make the diagnosis on contrast-enhanced MRI…' (caveat and note)"
      }
     ],
     "divergence": "Sources agree that hepatobiliary-contrast MRI sharpens the adenoma-versus-FNH distinction, but none makes it mandatory or addresses who may waive it, access or cost, and the audit's effect size is confounded.",
     "proposal": "Contrast-enhanced MRI for every patient, with a hepatobiliary agent whenever the question is adenoma versus FNH and always before pregnancy counselling; board triage returns an uncharacterised lesion for imaging rather than discussing it. The roadmap names no one who may waive the requirement.",
     "proposal_locator": "Gate 1 · Standard 'Make the diagnosis on contrast-enhanced MRI…'; Gate 6 · Standard 'Confirm the diagnosis on MRI with a hepatobiliary contrast agent…'; The board meeting · Triage",
     "rationale": "27% of referred sub-5 cm lesions proved to be focal nodular hyperplasia on hepatobiliary-contrast MRI in the prospective cohort — a lesion needing no follow-up at all. This is an access and cost question as much as a clinical one, so it needs a written answer rather than a preference.",
     "audit": "Audit: proportion characterised before first board discussion, and proportion whose diagnosis changed.",
     "web": "adenoma/#question-HCA-D1"
    },
    {
     "id": "HCA-D2",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "Does the programme adopt routine subtyping — a standing four-antibody panel on every specimen and biopsy, with CTNNB1 exon and codon sequencing where activation is suspected, and prostaglandin D synthase for the sonic hedgehog subgroup?",
     "question": "Does the programme adopt routine subtyping — a standing four-antibody panel on every specimen and biopsy, with CTNNB1 exon and codon sequencing where activation is suspected, and prostaglandin D synthase for the sonic hedgehog subgroup?",
     "kind": "clinical",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "1 · Confirm diagnosis and subtype",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-1"
      },
      {
       "label": "3 · Adenoma in a male patient",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-3"
      },
      {
       "label": "10 · Assess transplant eligibility",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-10"
      }
     ],
     "positions": [
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "No justification for recommending histopathology or molecular subtyping as routine practice; biopsy may be considered within a benign-tumour MDT to exclude malignancy; imaging-based subtyping needs prospective validation first.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Reasonable 'Biopsy when the imaging is atypical…' (caveat); Gate 2 · Reasonable 'Where the subtype is known and low risk…' (caveat)"
      },
      {
       "source": "AISF 2022 (Italian multisociety)",
       "key": "pm36089523",
       "says": "Recommends biopsy wherever imaging cannot classify the subtype, specifically to find beta-catenin exon 3 and sonic hedgehog adenomas, but grades this very low quality (D), conditional.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Reasonable 'Biopsy when the imaging is atypical…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "ACG 2024",
       "key": "pm38958301",
       "says": "Reserves biopsy for cases where imaging is inconclusive (strong recommendation, low-quality evidence).",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Reasonable 'Biopsy when the imaging is atypical…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "Rebouissou 2016",
       "key": "pm27177928",
       "says": "Only 27% of 33 beta-catenin-mutated adenomas showed the strong homogeneous glutamine synthetase pattern and S45 lesions are hard to recognise; the authors call for revised immunohistochemical criteria or mutation screening.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Reasonable 'Where tissue exists, ask for the four-antibody panel…' (caveat)"
      },
      {
       "source": "WHO 6th ed., as reviewed (2026)",
       "key": "pm42678415",
       "says": "Every inflammatory adenoma should be stained for glutamine synthetase; no reliable routine marker exists for sonic hedgehog adenoma and molecular profiling remains the reference. The roadmap notes it recommends a strategy whose prerequisite it calls unavailable.",
       "locator": "Gate 1 · Standard subtype option 'Sonic hedgehog (shHCA)'; Gate 1 · Reasonable 'Where tissue exists, ask for the four-antibody panel…' (pref); What we are not sure about #9"
      }
     ],
     "divergence": "EASL rejects routine subtyping while the Italian guideline, the molecular literature and the local transplant protocol's molecular route presuppose it, and the two subtypes that matter most can be identified reliably only by sequencing.",
     "proposal": "Biopsy is reserved for atypical imaging, suspected transformation or a subtype that would change management. On tissue that exists, the board model makes the four-antibody panel standard and requests CTNNB1 exon and codon where activation is suspected, a transplant route may apply, or the patient is male. Whether to subtype routinely is left to the group.",
     "proposal_locator": "Gate 1 · Reasonable 'Biopsy when the imaging is atypical…' and 'Where tissue exists, ask for the four-antibody panel…'; Gate 3 · Standard 'Send every male adenoma for expert liver pathology review…'; The board meeting · Pathology; What we are not sure about #8",
     "rationale": "EASL 2016 states there is no justification for recommending routine subtyping. The molecular data since, and the local transplant protocol's own molecular route, both assume it is available. Those two positions cannot both be acted on. [PAYER]",
     "audit": "Registry: subtype assigned, method, concordance with the resection specimen.",
     "web": "adenoma/#question-HCA-D2"
    },
    {
     "id": "HCA-D3",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "How long may risk-factor modification run before a persistent lesion over 5 cm is treated — EASL's 6 months, or 12, or longer in a lesion that is still falling?",
     "question": "How long may risk-factor modification run before a persistent lesion over 5 cm is treated — EASL's 6 months, or 12, or longer in a lesion that is still falling?",
     "kind": "clinical",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "2 · Risk-factor modification and reassessment",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-2"
      },
      {
       "label": "4 · Size, growth, and intervention",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-4"
      }
     ],
     "positions": [
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "Reassess every presumed adenoma with contrast MRI at 6 months; in women, resection is indicated for a nodule still 5 cm or more (II-3, grade 2). EASL also says no robust data define the timeline of stable disease.",
       "locator": "Gate 2 · Standard 'Reimage with contrast-enhanced MRI at 6 months…'; Gate 2 · Reasonable 'Extend observation past 12 months…' (note)"
      },
      {
       "source": "ACG 2024",
       "key": "pm38958301",
       "says": "Uses a looser 6- to 12-month imaging interval not tied to a size trigger (conditional, low quality), and holds that lesions of 5 cm or more warrant intervention.",
       "locator": "Gate 2 · Standard 'Reimage with contrast-enhanced MRI at 6 months…'; Gate 4 · Standard 'Treat a lesion that is persistently larger than 5 cm…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "AISF 2022 (Italian multisociety)",
       "key": "pm36089523",
       "says": "MRI 6 months after stopping contraception, repeated at 1 year if unchanged or reduced below 5 cm, then annual ultrasound after the first 18 months; every step, including the 6-month decision point, is graded very low quality (D), conditional.",
       "locator": "Gate 2 · Standard 'Reimage with contrast-enhanced MRI at 6 months…'; Gate 4 · Standard (caveat)"
      },
      {
       "source": "Klompenhouwer 2017",
       "key": "pm28857134",
       "says": "Of 194 patients with adenomas over 5 cm, 8.5-15.3% had regressed below 5 cm at 26 weeks against 58.5% eventually (median 104 weeks), without complications. The authors propose 12 months for typical non-beta-catenin adenomas in women. 47 of 241 were excluded for short follow-up.",
       "locator": "Gate 2 · Standard 'Reimage with contrast-enhanced MRI at 6 months…' (caveat and note); Gate 2 · Reasonable 'Extend observation past 12 months…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "European practice survey",
       "key": "pm36781070",
       "says": "48 of 61 responding experts (79%) advise against routine resection above 5 cm after lifestyle change; the roadmap notes a 21% response rate and stated rather than observed practice.",
       "locator": "Gate 2 · Avoid 'Do not operate on a large adenoma at 6 months…'"
      }
     ],
     "divergence": "EASL anchors the decision at 6 months, while ACG uses a looser 6- to 12-month interval. The regression cohort, its authors and most surveyed experts favour waiting longer. The authors' 12-month proposal ignores baseline diameter even though time to regression was strongly size-dependent.",
     "proposal": "Reimage with contrast MRI at 6 months and read it as a trajectory; do not call risk-factor modification a failure before 12 months (grade C, a deliberate deviation from EASL). Observation beyond 12 months is Reasonable in a woman with a typical, non-beta-catenin adenoma that is shrinking. No diameter-specific interval is proposed.",
     "proposal_locator": "What we are not sure about #4; Gate 2 · Standard 'Reimage with contrast-enhanced MRI at 6 months…'; Gate 2 · Reasonable 'Extend observation past 12 months…'",
     "rationale": "EASL and ACG indicate resection at 6 months. At that point only 8.5-15.3% of adenomas over 5 cm have fallen below 5 cm, against 23.7-27.1% at 52 weeks and 58.5% eventually at a median 104 weeks, with no complication during the whole of that follow-up and time to regression strongly size-dependent (median 63 weeks under 7 cm, 208 weeks over 10 cm). The six-month trigger therefore captures roughly one in six to one in ten of the lesions that will eventually regress below 5 cm. The cohort's authors proposed 12 months for typical non-beta-catenin adenomas in women; 79% of responding European experts already decline the rule; and the only GRADE-appraised guideline grades the 6-month decision point very low quality, conditional. Decide the number, and decide whether it differs by baseline diameter.",
     "audit": "Audit: time to fall below 5 cm or to intervention, for every adenoma over 5 cm under surveillance.",
     "web": "adenoma/#question-HCA-D3"
    },
    {
     "id": "HCA-D4",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "Does the programme resect every male adenoma irrespective of size, and is expert liver pathology review with sequencing mandatory in every male case?",
     "question": "Does the programme resect every male adenoma irrespective of size, and is expert liver pathology review with sequencing mandatory in every male case?",
     "kind": "clinical",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "3 · Adenoma in a male patient",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-3"
      }
     ],
     "positions": [
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "Resection or curative treatment for every adenoma in a man, irrespective of size, because of a higher incidence of malignant transformation (II-3, grade 2).",
       "locator": "Gate 3 · Standard 'Resect, irrespective of size…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "AISF 2022 (Italian multisociety)",
       "key": "pm36089523",
       "says": "Also recommends resection in men, as does ACG 2024, but grades it very low quality of evidence (D), conditional.",
       "locator": "Gate 3 · Standard 'Resect, irrespective of size…'; Gate 3 · Reasonable option 'Resection'"
      },
      {
       "source": "Farges 2011",
       "key": "pm21148580",
       "says": "Tertiary series of 218 patients: transformation in 47% of men against 4% of women; transformed lesions had a median 10 cm diameter. The roadmap reads 47% as a near-maximum referral figure, not a population risk.",
       "locator": "Gate 3 · Standard 'Resect, irrespective of size…' (caveat and note)"
      },
      {
       "source": "Dutch male series",
       "key": "pm34155783",
       "says": "Expert revision plus sequencing changed the diagnosis in 33 of 66 men (50%) and found 4 beta-catenin mutations missed by immunohistochemistry and re-review; the roadmap notes no comparator and mismatched mortality denominators.",
       "locator": "Gate 3 · Standard 'Send every male adenoma for expert liver pathology review…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "Haring 2022 resection audit",
       "key": "pm36324268",
       "says": "78% of men resected for a presumed adenoma had a changed final diagnosis, and male sex independently predicted a change (aOR 3.7).",
       "locator": "Gate 3 · Standard 'Send every male adenoma for expert liver pathology review…'"
      }
     ],
     "divergence": "The guidelines concur on resecting all men, but the transformation figures come from selected surgical series in which sex and size cannot be separated, and the male diagnosis is itself unreliable, so the rule may rest on risk or on diagnostic doubt.",
     "proposal": "Resect every male adenoma irrespective of size (grade C), treating it as possibly already malignant, and send every male case, including outside diagnoses, for expert liver pathology review with sequencing before accepting the diagnosis (grade D). Observing a sequenced, confidently low-risk lesion in a prohibitive operative candidate is a documented exception requiring a board decision.",
     "proposal_locator": "Gate 3 · Standard 'Resect, irrespective of size…' and 'Send every male adenoma for expert liver pathology review…'; Gate 3 · Reasonable option 'Observation with a sequenced, confidently low-risk subtype'; What we are not sure about #12-13",
     "rationale": "EASL, ACG and the Italian guideline all say resect, the last of them at very low quality of evidence, conditional. The male evidence is a 47% versus 4% transformation prevalence in one tertiary surgical series whose transformed lesions had a median diameter of 10 cm, an approximately ten-fold male excess in a second, and male sex as an independent predictor at an odds ratio of 18.9 (8.5-44.2). Against that, the diagnostic label is unreliable in exactly this group: 50% of presumed male adenomas are reclassified on expert review with 4 beta-catenin mutations found only by sequencing, and 78% of male resections in a national audit had their diagnosis changed. The full-text read also withdrew the 23.8% occult-carcinoma figure this page previously used — the supported figure is 4 transformations in 27 male adenomas — and found no cohort supporting observation of any male adenoma. So the question is whether the rule is retained because men are at higher risk or because the diagnosis cannot be trusted in men; the answer changes what the pathology requirement is for.",
     "audit": "Registry: sex, size, subtype, sequencing result, decision, specimen finding.",
     "web": "adenoma/#question-HCA-D4"
    },
    {
     "id": "HCA-D5",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "Is elective transarterial embolisation accepted as a first-line alternative to resection in a good surgical candidate who prefers it?",
     "question": "Is elective transarterial embolisation accepted as a first-line alternative to resection in a good surgical candidate who prefers it?",
     "kind": "clinical",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "4 · Size, growth, and intervention",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-4"
      },
      {
       "label": "7 · Multiple adenomas and adenomatosis",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-7"
      }
     ],
     "positions": [
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "Embolisation is an alternative to resection for larger lesions but the treatment of choice only in poor surgical candidates; residual viable lesion afterwards is an indication for resection.",
       "locator": "Gate 4 · Standard option group, 'Transarterial embolisation'; Gate 7 · Reasonable 'Transarterial embolisation of the dominant lesions…' (caveat)"
      },
      {
       "source": "TAE systematic review",
       "key": "pm28518415",
       "says": "Elective embolisation avoided surgery in 41 of 49 patients (84%); the roadmap notes small mixed series, a process endpoint, a 31-month pooled follow-up and no separate elective complication rate.",
       "locator": "Gate 4 · Standard option group, 'Transarterial embolisation'; What we are not sure about #16"
      },
      {
       "source": "TAE multicentre",
       "key": "pm31313827",
       "says": "In 59 patients median diameter fell from 76 to 48 mm, but grade D complications occurred in 10%, one patient died three months later, 34% still needed surgery and symptoms did not improve (P=0.134).",
       "locator": "Gate 4 · Standard option group, 'Transarterial embolisation' (caveat); What we are not sure about #16"
      }
     ],
     "divergence": "EASL confines embolisation to poor surgical candidates; later series report frequent surgery avoidance and shrinkage, but with a reported death, a third still operated, no symptom benefit and no histology.",
     "proposal": "Embolisation stays on the page (grade C) for a large, central or bilobar lesion where resection would be major, or a patient who wants to avoid an operation and accepts imaging follow-up, with harms disclosed in consent; in multiple adenomas its use is recorded as a departure from EASL. The roadmap does not itself endorse or decline first-line elective use.",
     "proposal_locator": "Gate 4 · Standard option group, 'Transarterial embolisation'; Gate 7 · Reasonable 'Transarterial embolisation of the dominant lesions…'; What we are not sure about #16",
     "rationale": "EASL reserves embolisation for poor surgical candidates. The data since show 84% surgery avoidance after elective embolisation, on 49 patients from multiple small series, and a fall in median diameter from 76 to 48 mm. Against that: 10% grade D complications, one death three months after emergency TAE for haemorrhagic shock followed by aspiration pneumonia, with uncertain causal attribution, 34% of patients still requiring surgery, an uncertain symptom effect at three months (58% symptomatic before versus 37% after; P=.134), viable tissue and no histology left behind in a disease where 20-40% of presumed adenomas prove to be something else, and a pooled follow-up of 31 months too short to detect transformation. That is a shared decision the programme should endorse or decline explicitly, with the harms disclosed, rather than leave to individual preference.",
     "audit": "Registry: modality, diameter change, complication grade, subsequent intervention.",
     "web": "adenoma/#question-HCA-D5"
    },
    {
     "id": "HCA-D6",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "What is the acute haemorrhage pathway, and who owns it out of hours?",
     "question": "What is the acute haemorrhage pathway, and who owns it out of hours?",
     "kind": "governance",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "5 · Acute bleeding or rupture",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-5"
      }
     ],
     "positions": [
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "Embolise the haemodynamically unstable bleeding adenoma (evidence level III, grade 2); resuscitate and transfer to a centre that can embolise; admit for observation and contrast CT when haemorrhage is clinically evident.",
       "locator": "Gate 5 · Standard 'Resuscitate with blood products…' and 'Admit and observe the stable patient…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "EASL 2016 (pregnancy)",
       "key": "pm27085809",
       "says": "Before 24 weeks, surgery may be preferred to embolisation for smaller peripheral resections because of fetal radiation and contrast exposure; in an exsanguinating patient the fastest route wins, per the roadmap.",
       "locator": "Gate 5 · Avoid 'Do not embolise a pregnant patient before 24 weeks…'"
      },
      {
       "source": "Huurman 2010",
       "key": "pm20357452",
       "says": "Review proposing the acute algorithm: practice has shifted from resection to initial conservative management with selective embolisation. The roadmap cites it from abstract only and calls this gate weakly sourced.",
       "locator": "Gate 5 · Standard 'Resuscitate with blood products…' (caveat); Provenance"
      },
      {
       "source": "TAE systematic review",
       "key": "pm28518415",
       "says": "Surgery was avoided in 68 of 151 embolised patients (45%), a figure mixing elective and acute indications; the roadmap says who needs a second procedure is identified by re-imaging.",
       "locator": "Gate 5 · Standard 'Re-image once stable…'"
      }
     ],
     "divergence": "Sources agree on embolisation first in the unstable patient, but only at evidence level III with no comparative series, and none defines the re-imaging interval, the transfer route or out-of-hours ownership.",
     "proposal": "Resuscitate, obtain contrast CT and transfer to an embolisation-capable centre; admit and observe contained bleeding without acute surgery; re-image once stable (MRI at 6-12 weeks, a convention) and resect residual viable lesion. Limited resection is reasonable for a small peripheral exophytic lesion where no embolisation exists. A same-day board route exists; no out-of-hours owner is named.",
     "proposal_locator": "Gate 5 · Standards 'Resuscitate with blood products…', 'Admit and observe the stable patient…', 'Re-image once stable…' and Reasonable 'Where the bleeding lesion is exophytic, peripheral and small…'; The board meeting · Urgent referral route",
     "rationale": "Not an evidence question. EASL's recommendation to embolise the unstable patient is evidence level III, and the practical failure mode is a young woman arriving at a hospital that cannot embolise, with no named transfer route. The interval to the post-embolisation re-imaging that decides whether she needs a resection is also undefined by any study.",
     "audit": "Audit: haemorrhage events with size, position, management route, time to definitive control, and whether residual viable lesion was resected.",
     "web": "adenoma/#question-HCA-D6"
    },
    {
     "id": "HCA-D7",
     "set": "roadmap",
     "title": "Does the programme adopt a composite operative trigger — 5 cm, or 20% growth, or exophytic position, or visible lesional arteries, or a segment II-III location — in place of diameter alone, and who will verify the figures behind it?",
     "question": "Does the programme adopt a composite operative trigger — 5 cm, or 20% growth, or exophytic position, or visible lesional arteries, or a segment II-III location — in place of diameter alone, and who will verify the figures behind it?",
     "kind": "clinical",
     "status": "OPEN — not yet decided",
     "where": [
      {
       "label": "4 · Size, growth, and intervention",
       "url": "adenoma/#decision-4"
      },
      {
